+{"verdict":"FAIL","summary":"Correctness FAIL, style PASS, security PASS, test_coverage FAIL, performance PASS, error_handling FAIL. The core Eq. 4, Eq. 3, Eq. 19, and 0/1 entropy-boundary formulas look mathematically correct in `gll_renner.py`, but the patch then exposes `ℓ/(n+m)` as a `bit/signal` rate even though the rest of the repo uses `bit/signal` to include the BB84 sifting factor `p_sift`; that overstates reported rates and drives the inflated Fig. 3-style snapshot. Separately, `eps_EC` and `eps_PA` are never validated, so invalid probabilities either raise raw runtime exceptions or silently make the key length more optimistic. The tests cover formula scaling and some sanity checks, but they do not actually prove cross-`N` monotonicity or near-threshold behavior, and they currently lock in the wrong rate normalization. I could not rerun `pytest` in this sandbox because no writable temp directory is available.","issues":[{"severity":"major","category":"correctness","location":"qkdx/finite_key/gll_renner.py:185","description":"`bb84_finite_key_rate_analytic()` returns `ell / (n + m)` but documents that as a per-signal rate. For BB84, `n` and `m` are post-selection block sizes, while repo-wide `bit/signal` accounting includes the sifting probability (`p_sift`, e.g. `p_Z^2 + (1-p_Z)^2` for efficient BB84). As written, the wrapper reports a per-accepted-round rate, not a per-transmitted-signal rate, and the accompanying snapshot/test assumptions inflate the advertised rate (for `p_Z=0.9`, roughly `0.3436` vs about `0.2809` when normalized by total signals).","suggestion":"Either rename this API to make it explicitly per-postselected-round/per-block, or add an explicit `p_sift`/`total_signals` parameter and compute `ell / N_total` so it matches the rest of the codebase’s `bit/signal` convention. Update the tests and Phase 1 log snapshot accordingly."},{"severity":"major","category":"error_handling","location":"qkdx/finite_key/gll_renner.py:178","description":"`bb84_finite_key_length_analytic()` validates `eps_PE` and `eps_bar` indirectly, but it never validates `eps_EC` or `eps_PA`. Values `<= 0` currently bubble out as raw `ZeroDivisionError`s, while values `> 1` are accepted and reduce or even invert the fixed penalties, producing nonsensical and overly optimistic key lengths.","suggestion":"Validate `eps_EC` and `eps_PA` in `(0, 1)` before computing the log terms, and add regression tests for zero/negative and greater-than-one inputs. Consider giving `total_security_parameter()` the same validation so the public API is consistent."},{"severity":"major","category":"test_coverage","location":"tests/test_finite_key/test_gll_renner.py:135","description":"The new tests do not actually verify some of the behaviors called out in the review scope. `test_bb84_finite_key_length_monotone_in_N` checks each `N` independently instead of asserting pairwise monotonic improvement, the threshold test only probes `e=0.20` rather than the actual transition region near the BB84 threshold, and the rate/snapshot tests encode the same `n+m == total signals` normalization mistake as the implementation.","suggestion":"Rewrite the `N` test to compare multiple sizes in one assertion, add near-threshold sign-change tests around the expected crossing region, add invalid-`epsilon` tests, and change the rate/snapshot checks to use explicit total-signal or `p_sift` normalization."}],"round_recommendation":"Fix the rate-normalization/API semantics first, then add explicit `eps_EC`/`eps_PA` validation and tighten the finite-key tests around true per-signal accounting, cross-`N` monotonicity, and near-threshold behavior."}
